Excellent — the download tool tests are comprehensive. Now let me plan the settle-loop cap enforcement (thread 12):

**RED test**: a download where the probe returns null (or a small value) but the settled item's `fileSize` exceeds `defaultMaxDownloadBytes` → the tool must cancel the download and throw `download_too_large`. Currently `_awaitDownloadSettled` just returns the item; the tool reports `ok: true` with size > cap. 

Implementation:
```dart
Future<DownloadItem?> _awaitDownloadSettled(int id) async {
  const budget = Duration(seconds: 10);
  const step = Duration(milliseconds: 250);
  final deadline = DateTime.now().add(budget);
  var last = await _findDownload(id);
  while (last != null && last.state == 'in_progress') {
    if (last.fileSize > defaultMaxDownloadBytes) {
      // In-flight cap enforcement (F2′): the Content-Length pre-check
      // cannot see chunked/opaque responses, so the observed expected
      // total is the enforcement point mid-flight.
      await _chrome.downloads.cancel(id);
      throw BrowserApiToolException(
        'download_too_large',
        'download $id reports ${last.fileSize} bytes — over the '
        '$defaultMaxDownloadBytes-byte download cap; cancelled',
      );
    }
    if (DateTime.now().isAfter(deadline)) break;
    await Future<void>.delayed(step);
    last = await _findDownload(id);
  }
  return last;
}
```

Wait — but should the check also run when the item settles complete with fileSize > cap? Chrome's fileSize is the total expected size — if the download completes, the full file is on disk; cancel is moot. But the FakeChrome.completeDownload sets fileSize at completion. If complete with fileSize > cap — the damage is done (file downloaded); reporting `ok` with size > cap is misleading. The reviewer's ask: "inside `_awaitDownloadSettled`, when `item.fileSize > defaultMaxDownloadBytes`, `await _chrome.downloads.cancel(id)` and surface `download_too_large` instead of the in-progress note."

Hmm, the reviewer's phrase "instead of the in-progress note" — they mean: the two windows are (1) unknown length proceeds uncapped, (2) the 10s wait gives up and reports in_progress while the download keeps running uncapped. Enforcing inside the loop with cancel covers both: even if the wait times out at 10s, DURING those 10s each poll checks fileSize, and after the loop... wait, if the loop breaks on deadline, the download keeps running and the tool reports in_progress — still uncapped!

To fully close window 2, I could also check once right before returning (after loop exit). But once the wait returns, we can't keep watching — the tool returns. The reviewer said: "Both close with the polling loop you already have: inside `_awaitDownloadSettled`, when `item.fileSize > cap`, cancel and surface `download_too_large`". So check on every poll iteration, including the last item fetched. Let me structure:

```dart
Future<DownloadItem?> _awaitDownloadSettled(int id) async {
  const budget = Duration(seconds: 10);
